  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nFor Sunday night, there are indications of a short wave impulse\nmoving southeast from the Upper Midwest into the western Great\nLakes around the periphery of the heat dome. The nose of the LLJ\nmoisture transport is more focused northeast of the area, \nalthough only slightly, and the CAM trend has been lesser\ncoverage on Sunday night. Kinematics are conducive for \norganization (clusters or possibly a complex) of storms where \nthey do develop and track. The timing though is challenging, and\ncannot rule out isolated activity Sunday evening due to the cap\npossibly being overcome in southern Wisconsin/northern \nIllinois. All in all continue the chance of storms, and \ntightened the PoP gradient on the western and southern edge.\n\nFor Monday, apart from morning convective influences, the\npattern is a favorable one for very high heat indices \n(105-110), as characterized by: 1.) 925 mb temperatures of \n25-30C, climatologically favoring at least mid 90s when well-\nmixed and no convective influences, 2.) widespread dew point \nreservoir of mid to upper 70s at peak evapotranspiration season,\n3.) very warm starting point to the day, and 4.) strong\nsuppression / capping signal for the afternoon. It is \nincreasingly likely (>70% chance) with this setup that an \nAdvisory or Warning will be needed for all counties on Monday, \nand that some, maybe most of the area, will be under a \ncontinuation of the Warning. By Monday night, the forcing is not\ncoincident with the cold front, and capping is forecast to \ncontinue, so continue with low to non-mentionable PoPs in that \ntime.\n\nNo changes made to the below Tuesday through Friday discussion.\n\nFollowing the passage of storms with the cold front overnight \ninto Tuesday, high pressure will build in on the northeast \nperiphery of the upper level ridge. The cold front will help \nslightly lower heat indices into the mid-90s with the \nintroduction of a drier airmass, though this heat will remain \nthrough both Tuesday and Wednesday as we remain under the \ninfluence of the ridge. \n\nBy midweek, the greatest question is when the ridge retrogrades and \nbrings us back into the summertime storm track and grants us a \nreprieve from the heat.The majority of the guidance has this change \ntowards the end of the period around Thursday and Friday, though \nthere a few outliers that have it as early as Wednesday. With this \nreturn to the storm track, there is the potential for another system \nto pass through as soon as Friday, though changes in speed could \nhave it arrive earlier.",
